Steve Lionel <Steve.Lionel@digital.com> writes:
> It would be useful to know why you want to use Linux instead of DIGITAL UNIX
> on Alpha.

Because it's free, as in free speech.

Because I can read the source of every program I use and learn from it
and change it and customise the whole system in ways that just aren't
possible with commercial OSes.

Because there is a great community of knowledgeable pleasant people
built around it and we all help each other out and Linux is ours in a
way no commercial OS could ever be.

Because I don't get "ERROR: license not found" messages.

Because it's lean and mean and makes great use of the hardware (the
same can be said for Digital Unix).

Because I can and do run the exact same OS on other machines,
ARM and x86 based.  Even Sparc occasionally.

Because it will stick around.  It is worthwhile for me to invest time
with it since the knowledge I acquire will pay off for decades to
come.  Linux won't disappear at the whim of some marketing maven, as
commercial OSes do from time to time.


>Is price of the software the only issue?

Not by a long shot.  But I originally got into Alpha Linux just about
two years ago when my boss said, in essence: "We have budget X to buy
you a new workstation for your compute-intensive work.  Presumably
you'd like an Alpha.  Would you prefer a run-of-the-mill one with
Digital Unix or a souped-up kick-ass one with Linux?"

Actually Digital wouldn't sell us a machine with Linux, nor bare.  We
had to get it with WinNT.  On day one, we formatted the hard disk and
installed Linux by FTP.  I suppose that if it had turned out to not to
be up to much, we could have switched to D.U. at a later date.  We
never switched.


Now I'm looking around for a machine to use at home, with budget Y
(much less than X =:-)  It will be an Alpha and it will run Linux for
all the reasons given above.

Rob.



