> a compiler, and that the support costs wouldn't eat us alive.  (We are
> concerned about the idea that everyone's Linux system is different from
> everyone else's...)

$500 is very reasonable to places used to paying several thousand. If you want
this to happen (and it seems like you do), you have to go to the people who
will buy it. I think a number of them read this group, even though they haven't
spoken up. But I think a large portion of them are totally ignorant of this
group, of Linux's potential, and even of the alpha's potential. They are
happy to use whatever computing resources their dept. gives them, and will
just complain to their sysadmin if there isn't enough to go around. 

I'd love to see this happen. I'd love to see the Digital C compilers
available for alpha as well, since gcc and egcs are (apparently) so lacking.
I'm not it much of a position to help though. What I would suggest
that you do is make some contacts, or heck, go to some of the *many* large
physics conferences, and show your goods (alpha, DU + Fortran), and then
when they gripe about the price say "well.. there is always linux...".

I'm sure Compaq already knows the benifits of getting a foothold in the
educational market.. 

Paul





