On Thu, 31 Dec 1998 11:10:41 -0500, Steve Lionel <Steve.Lionel@digital.com> wrote:
> ...
> What would influence them in favor of the proposal is to know
> that there are a large number of customers who would pay a reasonable price
> ($500 is the figure most users I've corresponded with have centered on) for
> a compiler, and that the support costs wouldn't eat us alive.  (We are
> concerned about the idea that everyone's Linux system is different from
> everyone else's...)

Well, speaking for my research group, we would be interested
in a good F90 compiler for Linux/Alpha, and would certainly pay $500
for it (we paid more than that for the PGI compiler for Linux/Intel).

Right now we've got a cluster of Intel machines, but might consider
Alphas in the future as their floating point performance is certainly
appealing.  Unfortunately, our codes are F90, which requires good
compilers.  I'm guessing there are other many other groups in the same
situation.

One possible solution to the support issue (albeit one that would 
likely stir up some controversy) is to only "officially" support one
distribution, with RedHat being the likely choice, and leave others
as unsupported platforms.  I believe that this is the approach being
taken by other commercial Linux software vendors.

-jason

