Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> wrote:

[snip]

> This is rather exceptional.  On the SPECfp95 suite, on an EB164 based
> machine egcs-1.[01] gets about 60 % of the performance of the Digital
> Fortran compiled code.

> A factor of 3 would already be exceptional, but 10-15 times slower means
> something else is wrong (probably not even related to the compiler).

> Is the part of the code where most of the CPU time is spent easily
> identifiable ?  In that case it would be possible to reason about the
> strange slow-down.

The code in question has 2 sqrts and 4 other divides in a tight loop
w/ 7 multiplies and 7 adds.  There are 5 operands per iteration.
It's not surprising that compilers have trouble w/ it.  The amazing
part is how well DEC FORTRAN handles it.

Because of the circumstances under which I was working. I was unable 
to properly study the differences in code generation.  I made some
attempt to do so, but lost some files moving them between machines
and was not able to recover.  Once the DU SDK arrives I will be
able to conduct more thorough tests and will provide more details.

Note this was not production code, but a loop extracted from a
production code, so there really wasn't anything else going on.

While I did a fairly lengthy set of tests of NDP FORTRAN flag
combinations, I did not do this w/ g77.  I suspect that there 
are better choices (I used -O5 -fast).

I will certainly look into the difference pretty carefully
as soon as time permits.  I quite agree that it is rather
extreme.

Reg Beardsley
