dontspam_esci@fastlane.net wrote:

> Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> wrote:

[ ... Slowdown of a factor of 10-15 between Digital
      Fortran and g77 is rather extreme ... ]

> The code in question has 2 sqrts and 4 other divides in a tight loop
> w/ 7 multiplies and 7 adds.  There are 5 operands per iteration.
> It's not surprising that compilers have trouble w/ it.  The amazing
> part is how well DEC FORTRAN handles it.

Ah, that explains most of it:  SQRT.  The Alpha doesn't have a SQRT
instruction (until the 21264) so the compiler has to code a function
call.  Digital Fortran is known to optimise loops with SQRT's by
unrolling them until they contain a multiple-of-4 number of SQRTs and
then calling a special run-time library to compute the 4 SQRTs "at
once".  Furthermore, the standard SQRT in glibc is known to be rather
slow - there have been recent efforts to recode the math routines that
showed spectacular improvements in this area (Perhaps one of the authors
can chime in ?).

