In linux.dev.kernel witra <witra@softhome.net> wrote:
: Speaking of battery backed RAM, why go to the trouble of providing
: special RAM for the hard disk? Why not simply have battery backup for
: the main system RAM and let the disk caching software handle it?

The main system can crash. A dedicated controller or the harddisk
firmware is much less likely to crash. And on a reboot, most
computers DO erase all the main memory. You'd have to rewrite
the BIOS to not do that.

: No need to shutdown anymore. Just turn off the electricity. When you
: turn the power back on, the system comes alive in a fraction of a
: second. No more waiting for the boot-up. 

That's the theory ...

