I got the Microway compiler and eventually returned it.  It does a fair
job on some code and a poor job on others.  Unfortunately, the simple 
tests were quick and the real problem was slow (5x :-(

Interestingly in some cases g77 was quicker though for the real problem
g77 was a factor of 10-15 slower than DEC FORTRAN.

They don't have a debugger for NDP FORTRAN, so you have to use gdb
which is NOT easy w/ FORTRAN.

Writing a really good compiler is a lot of work.  DEC invested a lot
of money in the GEM compilers.  I find it amazing that people expect
g77 to come close.  I'm thrilled g77 exists and works at all. It's
really a good compiler.  It's just not highly optimized for the
alpha.  If speed matters there really is only one answer right now.
Hopefully, Compaq will recognize that they are in a wonderful position
to catch the Linux wave w/ high end clusters by selling a Linux port
of their development tools.

BTW The Microway Alphas are very nicely built.  Best machine I've
seen from someone other than DEC-SGI-IBM-Sun.  Excellent cabinet,
good peripherals and meticulous assembly. Cabling niely laid out
and tied.  Truly a quality product.

Have fun!

