This isn't my real e-mail address.  Please respond to the news group.

I don't think that there is a way to get egcs to generate asm with the C
code as comments.  You could write it? :^)

The Alpha is strictly a load-store architecture.  One of the
implications of that is that to do a memory-to-memory copy, you have to
load a value into a register and then store the contents of that
register to a new location.

As for the "fastest" way to do a large memcpy, that depends on
implementation. For example, if BWX is supported, the code may/will look
different.  Check the chapter on "Software Considerations" in the Alpha
Architecture Handbook to get a better idea of all the options.

For the unaligned prefix, you are probably better off loading the
naturallly aligned quadwords containing both the first source and first
target bytes, then shifting and masking before writing the result. After
that prefetch cache blocks and unroll the read/write loop to take
advantage of all the memory bandwidth.

The problem of course is dealing with arbitrarily aligned data. (See A.3
in the Alpha Architecture Handbook for more detail on the software
issues involved with read/write performance.)

Assuming that you want to move a block of 40 bytes (5 quad words) that:
   1.  is not naturally aligned
   2.  straddles a cacheline boundary
   3.  does not straddle a page boundary (don't want to worry
       about page faults)

To a block of 40 bytes that:
   1.  is not naturally aligned
   2.  does not have the same alignment within its quadword
   3.  straddles a cacheline boundary
   4.  does not straddle a page boundary
   5.  does not overlap with the source block
   6.  does not cause cache conflicts with the first block (is not
       mapped to the same cacheline as any part of the first block)

This gives you data that looks something like this:

   Src  = 11111aaa aaaaabbb bbbbbccc cccccddd dddddeee eeeee111
   Dest = 222yyyyy xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx zzz22222

Where:
   1:   is a byte value that should not be copied
   2:   is a byte value that should remain unchanged
   a, b, c, d, and e: are byte values that are copied
   x, y, and z: are byte values that will be overwritten

Then, the code does something similar to:

load the 1st Src quadword (11111aaa)
load the 1st Dest quadword (222yyyyy)
prefetch the first quadword of the next cacheline of Src
prefetch the first quadword of the next cacheline of Dest

Doing these 4 loads 1st ensures that we encounter as few waits for the
memory system as possible.  All of the desired memory is now being
loaded into the L1 cache.

load the 2nd Src  quadword (aaaaabbb)
load the 3rd Src  quadword (bbbbbccc)
load the 4th Src  quadword (cccccddd)
load the 5th Src  quadword (dddddeee)

(this gives us 8 aligned quadword loads including the 2 touches. Digital
recommends using no more than 8 consecutive aligned quadword loads to
avoid overwhelming the memory subsystem, so now do some calculation
before loading the last 2 quadwords)

Start by using the 1st value that was loaded. Don't use it immediately
after loading, because you need to allow for memory latency.

shift (11111aaa) to (aaa00000)
shift (aaa00000) back to (000aaa00)

shift (222yyyyy) to (00000222)
shift (00000222) back to (22200000)

shift (aaaaabbb) to (000000aa)
shift (aaaaabbb) to (aaabbb00)

AND (000000aa) and (000aaa00) to (000aaaaa)
AND (000aaaaa) ans (22200000) to (222aaaaa)
store (222aaaaa) to the 1st quadword of Dest

shift (bbbbbccc) to (000000bb)
shift (bbbbbccc) to (bbbccc00)

AND (aaabbb00) and (000000bb) to (aaabbbbb)
store (aaabbbbb) to the 2nd quadword of Dest

If this doesn't allow enough time since the previous 8 loads, move these
loads to a later slot.

load the 6th Src  quadword (eeeee111)
load the 6th Dest quadword (zzz22222)

continue shifting and storing until done.

Compare that code sequence to the code needed to do a memcpy between 2
non-overlapping blocks of memory that are aligned on 128 byte boundaries
(sufficient to ensure alignment with cache blocks for all current Alpha
implementations) and that have no cacheline conflicts.

prefetch 1st quadword of Dest
load 1st quadword of Src
load 2nd quadword of Src
load 3rd quadword of Src
load 4th quadword of Src
prefetch 5th quadword of Dest
load 5th quadword of Src

store 1st quadword to Dest
store 2nd quadword to Dest
store 3rd quadword to Dest
store 4th quadword to Dest
store 5th quadword to Dest

You can see that if the compiler follows the additional alignment
restrictions, the code can be much simpler/faster.  Of course this also
means that the compiler has to be smart enough to generate good code.

Additional considerations due to calling conventions:

With the Alpha architecture, you have 31 usable registers, so you can do
this kind of load/manipulate/store sequence without running out of
registers.  Of course, you have to keep in mind the calling conventions
of the OS you are using (does anybody know what they are on
Linux/Alpha?).  For example, 1 register is probably reserved for
function call return addresses, 1 for compiler use, 8 for values that
are maintained across function call boundaries, 8 for scratch use within
a function, 8 for parameter passing, ....  Even so, you can usually work
out a code sequence that gives good performance.

As an additional reference/case study, www.microway.com has a nice white
paper on the kind of optimizations their compilers use for floating
point code on Alpha.


