[This followup was posted to comp.os.linux.alpha and a copy was sent to 
the cited author.]

Here's an installation method that should work. As long as you don't lose 
the linux environment you should be able to carry this installation to 
completion, with aboot and linux on a single disk. 

I used the files and directions from:

  ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/ALPHA/alpha/azstarnet/jensen/install

I rely heavily on the vmlinux.gz there. Others I have tried don't boot to 
the login prompt for one reason or another. When making the ARC utilities 
disk, I also placed bootlx and vmlinux.gz there, plus swriteboot, which I 
got somewhere else. I'll use these files toward the end of the 
installation to fixup the boot disk.

Make a SRM console bootable disk for 'aboot' from the ARC Console: Use 
minlabel and copyboot to create bootable disk. In my system the disk is 
dka200, /dev/sdb. Switch to the SRM Console.

From the SRM Console: Boot aboot to interactive mode: 

	boot -fl "I" dka200

Use aboot to load the redhat ramdisk: 

	0/a root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=0

Perform the redhat installation up to the 'fdisk' option.

At the 'fdisk' option, choose 'fdisk' and create a partition table for 
the 'aboot' disk, /dev/sdb in my case, that doesn't use the first meg or 
so. I start the disk partitions in the second cylinder. Yes this is the 
point where your disk becomes temporarily unbootable.

Continue your installation to the last "OK" prompt. Before selecting "OK" 
to complete the installation, <ALT>-<F2> to a terminal session. Use fdisk 
to create a BSD partition partition table mirroring the one created 
during the install. Then mount the floppy with swriteboot, vmlinux.gz and 
bootlx. swriteboot became swritebo when copied to the floppy, since its a 
FAT floppy.

cd to the floppy and execute 'swritebo /mnt/dev/sdb bootlx vmlinux.gz', 
substituting the correct device for your 'aboot' disk as the first 
argument to swritebo. This is the step that makes the disk bootable 
again.

Good luck. I've done this, but I keep making little mistakes and haven't 
done it completely. Putting swriteboot, vmlinux and bootlx on the floppy 
was the step I left off. I made up for that error by mounting an already 
installed version of RedHat where I had these things, so I'm confident it 
will work. I'm going through it now to be sure.

--

