Hi,

I noticed that using

#include <huge_val.h>

float a=HUGE_VAL;

will give a floating point exception when compiled without `-mieee' with
gcc.

Gcc's man page says that compiling in IEEE conformity slows down
execution, because not
so much of the alpha's special floating point hardware can be used.

Shouldn't there be a resonable value in huge_val.h in case `-mieee' is
_not_ given on an alpha.
Like
#define HUGE_VAL 1e100
or whatever is near to the end of a normal float.

---david

