On Tue, 8 Dec 1998, Darren Mackay wrote:

> I had a Pentium133 running nt4 (yes, I know, but then again, I have had no
> problems with it locking up, ever!) that I was going to retire. A guy in
> another department had a WD 1G solid state disk that he no longer used
> (consists of memory modules with a battery backup). It now flies. Although,
> I admit that a disk array may outperform it, but the solid state 'disk' was
> a freebie.
> 
> BTW, I don't necessirily agree with a lot Dru's ideas, but I can agree the
> principle does work, when compared to a non-array disk system anyway.
> 
> Darren

This solid state disks are something entirely (well ... maybe not
_entirely_) different from the type of caching that Dru is talking about.
What Dru is talking about saves the disk writes for CPU idle time.  solid
state disks take the place of a HDD entirely.  They utilize the design of
memory chips in a way such that the only time anything has to be written
to any kind of physical media is when the computer is shut down.  In this
senario all "Hard Drive" access is hundreds of times faster just because
the media being written two has no moving parts.

