From: p.mott@mathworks.com (Peter Mott)
Subject: Re: Installing and testing an axpci33
Date: 1998/12/08
Message-ID: <MPG.10d74b52c86d6c70989698@professional_news.mathworks.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 420153237
References: <366D770F.86036769@draper.com>
Organization: The MathWorks, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

[This followup was posted to comp.os.linux.alpha and a copy was sent to 
the cited author.]

This is not the correct behavior to expect, although there is probably a 
perfectly good explanation.

You need at least 2 simms. The Alpha's memory bus is 64 bits wide,  32 
bits from each of two 72 pin true parity simms. When I did this with less 
than 16Mb, the SRM console never came up.

Your video card must be recognized by the firmware, or the console goes 
into serial console mode. 

Once you have satisfied the firmware's needs with these things you should 
see a bios emulation message on the screen within about 10 seconds.

Once you have gotten this far, you may not see anything else on the 
screen, which would mean the console is in serial mode. After about 30 
seconds hit <enter> and see if you get an SRM prompt, '>>>'. If so, 
you're on your way. 

Issue the console command 'show' to see the console environment. Issue 
the console command 'set console graphics' to set the console to graphics 
mode. At this point if you cycle power you should see more messages on 
the screen.

At this point, I installed the ARC console. If you want to keep the SRM 
console you'll need 'aboot' to continue.

Good luck. Hope something here proves valuable.
- Peter Mott


