Subject: Re: Dealing with unaligned accesses
Date: 1998/12/09
Message-ID: <366E7769.4B6FF2D@sktc.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 420419366
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <36673795.6C675A97@netcom.com>
X-Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: SouthWind Internet Access, Inc.
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

> access occurs.  The data item at that address is an eight byte pointer.
> The first byte of the structure is at address 2017fbd4.  This means that
> moving the pointer to the top of the structure won't help.  In fact, the
> structure is an even number of 64 bits in length.

First, re-order the structure so that it allocates the biggest items first:
pointers and arrays, then shorts, then chars.

Second, modify the malloc call to look something like this:

foo *my_pointer;
void *save_ptr;
save_ptr = malloc(sizeof(foo)+8);
my_pointer = (foo *)(((unsigned long)save_ptr+7)&(~7));

This will force the actual pointer to be qword aligned no matter what
malloc returns. There is one gotcha: you cannot free my_pointer, you must
free save_ptr.

Malloc ought to return aligned memory; perhaps there's a bug in libc/glibc.






