> the Linux kernel comes up and  after a while it stops with the message
> samething like:
>
> ncr53c810a-0-<4,0>:FAST-5 SCSI 4.0 MB/s ..........
> Device not ready. Make sure that is a disk in the drive
> ....
> ...
> CDROM I/O error : dev 0b:00 sector
> EXT2-fs : unable to read superblock
>
> Is my cdrom drive bad ??
> When i try to boot from a floppy its stops after the ramdisk
>
> Any idears ??

 I have the same machine you describe with dual boot:
OpenVMS and Alpha Linux (RedHat50), and with the
SRM console.

As I need to keep VMS I didn't touch the BIOS (call it
superstition if you want! :-).

What I did is to find the appropriate MILO image in the
CD and make a MILO diskette. It required some trial and
error, but finally one of the Avanti images worked.

Then I boot from the diskette with 'boot dva'. MILO does
a startup and presents you with a prompt. There you have
to declare how much memory you have with:

    set MEMORY_SIZE 128      (or whatever you have)

and then boot from the CD or from the hard disk. You can
verify that the CD is accessible with:

    MILO> ls -type iso9660 sr0:/kernels

(if I remember it correctly).

Once I have installed Linux I boot wiht 'boot dka0' when
I want to work with VMS or 'boot dva' (from MILO diskette)
when I want to work in Linux. Fortunately I don't have to
reboot too often!

Contact me if you have any further questions.

--
Carlos Vidal
