Mike Schroeder wrote:

> I have a spare Shark 56k modem which I'm hoping to be able to use in my
> AlphaStation 200.  This modem is "plug and play" on a PC.  Can RedHat
> 5.1 work with this?  What can I do to get Linux to "see" it?
>
> Mike Schroeder
> mike.s-at-earthling.net  (Does it really fool the Spamers to use a dummy
> address here???)

Mike:
This may help as I recently installed a 56k USR internal fax modem that was
also
plug and play on Red Hat 5.1 for alpha

Get the latest version of isapnp, try  http://www.roestock.demon.co.uk/
It will not compile out of the box, but if you check the error messages,
there
is an undeclared data type in
/usr/src/linux-2.0.34/include/asm-alpha/pyxis.h
You have to change it to unsigned long from _u long or whatever it is.
That should
allow the compile to proceed.

Then run pnpdump and comment out the hash marks in the file
/etc/isapnp.conf that will
give you the configuration you want.  Be sure to check that you get the
right interrupt.
For example, my modem is sitting at /dev/ttyS2 on IRQ 10 and 0x03e8.

If you want to have the modem wake up and answer the phone/fax, as opposed
to just
dialing out, then you will need a line like

# serial line to listen for modem
8:2345:respawn:/sbin/mgetty -n 3 -m '"" AT OK ATS0=0 OK ATV1 OK ATX4 OK
ATQ0 OK' /dev/ttyS2

in your /etc/inittab file. I don't know about the modem commands in your
case, so
some experimentation is called for.

Hope this is of some help.  Send me email if you have more questions

Cheers,

Larry Bates
bates@tiki.math.ucalgary.ca






